# Command: FV (Read and Answer frames)   -- TS-590 ONLY (no FV block exists in the
#   TS-480 book)
# Radio: Kenwood TS-590S / TS-590SG
# Title (cover, verbatim): "TS-590S TS-590SG / PC CONTROL COMMAND / Reference Guide"
# Printed revision: NONE printed; cover carries "JVCKENWOOD Corporation" and
#   "January/30/2019".
# Source page: printed folio "- 12 -"  ==  PDF page 13 of 35
# Charts used: the Read and Answer charts of the FV block (the block prints NO Set
#   chart - the row is absent, not empty)
# Derivation date: 05/09/2026
# NO code, no generator, no other file and no other document was consulted.
#
# COUNTED FRAME LENGTHS (two independent counts of the printed numbered ruler):
#   FV Read   = 3 positions
#   FV Answer = 7 positions
#
# CHARTS, position by position:
#   Read:    1 F  2 V  3 ;                                     (4..10 empty)
#   Answer:  1 F  2 V  3 P1  4 P1  5 P1  6 P1  7 ;             (8,9,10 empty)
#
# Field map:
#   pos 1-2  "FV"
#   pos 3-6  P1  (four characters)
#   pos 7    ";"
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEGEND, VERBATIM (the complete right-hand column; block description reads
# "Verifies the Firmware version."):
#
#   [TS-590S / TS-590SG common]
#   Parameters:
#   P1
#     Reads out the character string of the firmware version.
#
#   * For example, for firmware version 1.00, it reads "FV1.00;".
#
#   THE WORKED EXAMPLE, VERBATIM:  For example, for firmware version 1.00, it reads
#   "FV1.00;".
#   (The example is 7 characters long and so agrees exactly with the counted chart:
#    P1 = "1.00", four characters occupying positions 3,4,5,6, with the full stop
#    counted as one of the four.  Positions 3..6 therefore need NOT be digits.)
#
# POSITIONS THE CHART PRINTS AS FIXED / "Always": NONE in the FV block.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BYTES WHOSE VALUE THE MANUAL DOES NOT DETERMINE  -> INHERITED-ASSUMED
#   [V1] The four characters of P1 for any version other than the one in the worked
#        example.  The legend gives no format ("the character string of the firmware
#        version"), only the worked example fixes the shape "d.dd".  The two extra
#        answer vectors below extend that shape to other version numbers and their
#        exact bytes are therefore ASSUMED.
#   The "fv590_answer_v1_00" vector is NOT assumed: it is the book's own worked
#   example, character for character.
#
# Hardware status: UNVERIFIED for every vector in this file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VECTORS  (name <TAB> frame bytes verbatim)
#
fv590_read	FV;
# The book's own worked example, quoted character for character
fv590_answer_v1_00	FV1.00;
# Same shape, other versions - bytes ASSUMED, see [V1]
fv590_answer_v2_00	FV2.00;
fv590_answer_v1_08	FV1.08;
